---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import GradeCalculator from '@components/islands/GradeCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'grade-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I calculate my weighted grade?', answer: 'Multiply each assessment\'s score by its weight, add those up, and divide by the total weight. The "Weighted average" mode does this — add a row for each assignment, test or project with its score and weight.' },
  { question: 'How do I calculate a weighted average?', answer: 'Weight each score by its share of the grade. A quiz at 80% (worth 20%), a midterm at 75% (30%) and a final at 90% (50%) give 0.20×80 + 0.30×75 + 0.50×90 = 83.5%. The heavier the weight, the more that score moves the result.' },
  { question: 'What grade do I need on the final?', answer: 'Switch to "Final grade needed" mode. Enter your current grade, how much the final is worth, and your target grade, and the calculator tells you the score you need on the final.' },
  { question: 'What if the needed score is over 100%?', answer: 'It means your target is not reachable with the final alone. The calculator flags this so you can set a realistic goal.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between a course grade and a GPA?', answer: 'A course grade is your percentage in one class, weighted by assessments. A GPA converts final letter grades across several courses to points (on a 4.0 scale) and averages them by credit hours. Use the GPA calculator for the second.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-04' }}
  intro="Work out your weighted course grade from assignments and tests, or find exactly what you need to score on the final to reach your target."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <GradeCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Two grade tools in one</h2>
    <p>
      Switch between them with the mode control. <strong>Weighted average</strong> combines
      your scores by their weights to show your current grade. <strong>Final grade needed</strong>
      works backwards from a target: enter your current grade and how much the final is worth,
      and it returns the score you need on that final.
    </p>

    <h2>Calculating a weighted average</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose the <strong>weighted average</strong> mode.</li>
      <li>Add a <strong>row per assessment</strong> — a quiz, test, project or exam.</li>
      <li>Enter each item's <strong>score</strong> (as a percentage) and its <strong>weight</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The weights are treated as relative shares, so they do not have to add up to 100. The
      tool multiplies each score by its weight, sums those, and divides by the total weight —
      if your weights happen to total something other than 100, it normalises by that total
      automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Say a course is graded on a quiz worth 20%, a midterm worth 30% and a final worth 50%. If
      you scored 80% and 75% on the first two and expect 90% on the final, your grade is
      (0.20 × 80) + (0.30 × 75) + (0.50 × 90) = <strong>83.5%</strong>. Because the final carries
      half the weight, it moves your grade more than the quiz and midterm combined.
    </p>
    <p>
      Weights need not total 100. Score 90 on an item weighted 2 and 80 on an item weighted 1,
      and the grade is (90 × 2 + 80 × 1) ÷ 3 = <strong>86.67%</strong> — the same math, just
      normalised by the total weight of 3.
    </p>

    <h2>What you need on the final</h2>
    <p>
      The second mode answers "what must I score on the final?" Enter your current grade, the
      final's weight as a percentage of the whole course, and your target overall grade. Writing
      the final's weight as a fraction <em>w</em>, the required score is:
    </p>
    <p><strong>needed = (target − (1 − w) × current) ÷ w</strong></p>
    <p>
      For example, with a <strong>current grade of 88%</strong>, a final worth <strong>25%</strong>
      (so <em>w</em> = 0.25) and a <strong>target of 90%</strong>, you need (90 − 0.75 × 88) ÷ 0.25
      = 24 ÷ 0.25 = <strong>96%</strong> on the final.
    </p>

    <h2>When the target isn't reachable</h2>
    <p>
      Sometimes the required score comes out above 100%, which means the target cannot be reached
      with the final alone. Aiming for <strong>85%</strong> from a <strong>70% current grade</strong>
      with the final worth <strong>40%</strong> needs (85 − 0.6 × 70) ÷ 0.4 = <strong>107.5%</strong>
      — impossible, so the tool flags it as not reachable. If instead the number comes out at or
      below zero, you have already secured the target regardless of the final.
    </p>

    <h2>Understanding weights</h2>
    <p>
      Assessments rarely count equally — a final exam might be worth 30% while a quiz is 5%.
      Weighting reflects that, so a high score on a heavily weighted item matters most, and it is
      why the final so often decides a borderline grade. To combine final grades across whole
      courses into a GPA, use the <a href="/everyday/gpa-calculator">GPA calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
